{{> head}}
{{> nav}}
<main id="main">

  <section class="section hero scheme-dark case-hero" aria-labelledby="case-title">
    <div class="container hero__inner">
      <div class="hero__copy">
        <div class="stack">
          <p class="eyebrow" data-reveal>Case study · Landing page</p>
          <h1 class="h1" id="case-title" data-split>A waitlist page built to <span class="mark">convert<svg class="mark__svg" viewBox="0 0 240 24" preserveAspectRatio="none" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false" data-draw><path d="M4 15 C 46 8, 92 19, 136 12 S 210 7, 236 13"/></svg></span></h1>
          <p class="text-lg" data-reveal>The pre-launch page for Tales AI, the bedtime-story app that launched as Lula. Designed for one action, joining the waitlist, and set in one storybook world illustrated with Nano Banana.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <dl class="facts" data-reveal>
        <div><dt>Client</dt><dd>Horus Studios</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Our role</dt><dd>Landing page design and front-end build</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Built with</dt><dd>Next.js, Tailwind CSS, GSAP</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Code</dt><dd><a class="text-link" href="https://github.com/basakfeyz/tales-ai-landingpage" target="_blank" rel="noopener">On GitHub</a></dd></div>
      </dl>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section scheme-light" aria-label="Landing page hero">
    <div class="container">
      <figure class="shot" data-reveal>
        <img src="/assets/img/work/tales/hero-1920.webp" srcset="/assets/img/work/tales/hero-960.webp 960w, /assets/img/work/tales/hero-1920.webp 1920w" sizes="(min-width: 1440px) 1280px, 92vw" width="1920" height="1200" alt="Tales AI landing page hero: the headline Your voice, Their adventure, an email field with a Join the Waitlist button, and a grid of six storybook illustrations." loading="lazy">
        <figcaption>Above the fold: the promise, one email field, one button, and the product's world.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section scheme-light" aria-labelledby="cro-title">
    <div class="container ai-def">
      <h2 class="h2" id="cro-title" data-split>Designed for conversion</h2>
      <div class="text-lg prose" data-reveal>
        <p>A waitlist page has one job. Every section either builds the case for signing up or removes a reason not to.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Sign-up above the fold.</strong> The hero asks for an email address only. The lowest-friction ask comes first.</li>
          <li><strong>One call to action, repeated.</strong> "Join the Waitlist" sits in the navigation, the hero, a dedicated waitlist block and the closing section, always the same words and the same gold button.</li>
          <li><strong>Benefits before features.</strong> "Your voice. Their adventure." leads; the feature cards follow and each one answers "what does my child get?".</li>
          <li><strong>Objections answered before they stop a sign-up.</strong> The FAQ covers what parents worry about: how personalisation works, using their own voice, children's data, special needs and tracking progress.</li>
          <li><strong>A reason to act now.</strong> Early access for the first 1,000 families.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section scheme-dark" aria-labelledby="imagery-title">
    <div class="container split-rows">
      <div class="ai-def">
        <h2 class="h2" id="imagery-title" data-split>AI imagery, art-directed into one world</h2>
        <div class="text-lg prose" data-reveal>
          <p>Every illustration on the page was generated with Nano Banana (Gemini). The craft is in making them look like they came from one storybook: the same midnight-navy sky and warm gold light, the same soft painterly finish, the same children and animal friends from scene to scene.</p>
          <p>That consistency matters more for a children's product than for most. Parents are wary of AI content for their kids, so the imagery has to feel warm and cared for, never generic. The same thinking carried into the app's hand-made illustration style.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="shots" data-reveal-group>
        <figure class="shot" data-reveal>
          <img src="/assets/img/work/tales/features-1920.webp" srcset="/assets/img/work/tales/features-960.webp 960w, /assets/img/work/tales/features-1920.webp 1920w" sizes="(min-width: 1440px) 1280px, 92vw" width="1920" height="1160" alt="Feature section Stories shaped by you, with illustrated cards: a parent recording a story, a child sailing as the hero, and a bear and owl as narrators." loading="lazy">
          <figcaption>Feature cards: each benefit gets its own scene.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure class="shot" data-reveal>
          <img src="/assets/img/work/tales/narrate-1920.webp" srcset="/assets/img/work/tales/narrate-960.webp 960w, /assets/img/work/tales/narrate-1920.webp 1920w" sizes="(min-width: 1440px) 1280px, 92vw" width="1920" height="1200" alt="Narrate section: Choose how the story gets told, with an owl reading a book and a parent recording into a microphone for her child." loading="lazy">
          <figcaption>Narration: a parent's voice or a storybook character.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure class="shot" data-reveal>
          <img src="/assets/img/work/tales/cta-1920.webp" srcset="/assets/img/work/tales/cta-960.webp 960w, /assets/img/work/tales/cta-1920.webp 1920w" sizes="(min-width: 1440px) 1280px, 92vw" width="1920" height="1624" alt="Closing section Start creating today, with a Join the Waitlist button above an illustration of a mother and child reading on a crescent moon." loading="lazy">
          <figcaption>The close: the same button, the warmest image.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section scheme-light" aria-labelledby="build-title">
    <div class="container ai-def">
      <h2 class="h2" id="build-title" data-split>Designed and built</h2>
      <div class="text-lg prose" data-reveal>
        <p>We built the page ourselves in Next.js and Tailwind CSS, with GSAP for the scroll reveals and a quiet night sky of drifting stars. Design and code came from the same hands, so nothing got lost between the two.</p>
        <p>That is what we mean by <a class="text-link" href="/design-engineering/">design engineering</a>. The page was the first public face of a product that later launched as <a class="text-link" href="/work/lula/">Lula</a>.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  {{> work-next}}
</main>
{{> footer}}
